-- title:
--   Elements of $\mathbb{C}[X_0,\dots,X_{n-1}]$ that are Laurent polynomials in $F_n+1$ lie in $\mathbb{C}[F_n]$
-- statement:
--   Fix $n\ge4$ and let $A_n=\mathbb{C}[X_0,\dots,X_{n-1}]$. With $h=X_0$, $u=X_1$, $v=X_2$, $w=X_3$, $x=u^3+hv$, $y=-u^2+hw$, $s=2u^3v+3u^4w+h(v^2-3u^2w^2)+h^2w^3$ and $p=-2s^2x+3sy^2-3s^3y$, let $F_n=h-p-1\in A_n$; it involves only the first four variables. Let $\mathbb{C}[F_n]\subseteq A_n$ be the $\mathbb{C}$-subalgebra generated by $F_n$, i.e. the set of all $g(F_n)$ with $g\in\mathbb{C}[t]$, and for $q\in\mathbb{C}[t]$ write $q(F_n+1)\in A_n$ for $q$ evaluated at $F_n+1$.
--
--   Let $L$ be any commutative ring with an $A_n$-algebra structure $\lambda:A_n\to L$ that makes $L$ a localization of $A_n$ away from $F_n+1$. Then for every $a\in A_n$,
--
--   $$
--   \Bigl(\exists\,m\in\mathbb{N}\ \ \exists\,q\in\mathbb{C}[t]:\quad \lambda(F_n+1)^m\,\lambda(a)=\lambda\bigl(q(F_n+1)\bigr)\Bigr)\iff a\in\mathbb{C}[F_n].
--   $$
--
--   The left-hand side says that the image of $a$ in $L\cong A_n[(F_n+1)^{-1}]$ is a Laurent polynomial in $F_n+1$, namely $q(F_n+1)/(F_n+1)^m$. So the theorem says that the Laurent polynomials in $F_n+1$ meet $A_n$ exactly in the ordinary polynomials in $F_n$: no negative power of $F_n+1$ survives in an element of $A_n$. This is the ring-theoretic core of identifying the common kernel of the commuting derivations with $\mathbb{C}[F_n]$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $F_n$ is `extendedF hn` and $F_n+1$ is `extendedAmbientParameter hn`. The ring $L$ is an arbitrary type with instances `[Algebra (MvPolynomial (Fin n) ℂ) L]` and `[IsLocalization.Away (extendedAmbientParameter hn) L]`; $q(F_n+1)$ is `Polynomial.aeval (extendedAmbientParameter hn) q`, and $\mathbb{C}[F_n]$ is `Algebra.adjoin ℂ {extendedF hn}`.
